# Review package: batch 6, slice sql

## 38e531ae Parse the orchestrator's SQL with Postgres's own parser, ahead of the tool that will call it

This is the sql-check part of Plan 3 Task 3, split into its own lane. The check stands between the orchestrator's text and
the database and sees what the database's rights cannot, such as COMMIT or SET, or a function that runs query text. Nothing
calls checkAgentSql yet; confirm.ts, agent-sql.ts, client.ts, database.ts, the errors and the texts stay with that task's
writer. REFUSED_FUNCTIONS is exported from here as the one list, for that writer's PUBLIC_REVOKES to import, so there is no
second copy. Adds libpg-query at exactly 17.7.3, which brings @pgsql/types; the lane's hard-linked node_modules went first
and npm ci ran, by ruling 7 of the parallel map.

The walk goes over every key of the parser's tree, so a node kind nobody listed cannot hide a call: nested subqueries, CTEs,
function arguments, the SET of an UPDATE, ON CONFLICT, RETURNING and the JSON and XML table forms each have a case. Text a
function would run as SQL is refused by the function's name and never parsed.

Proved by test/sql-check.test.ts, seen red first. The first green attempt also showed one bug of its own: an INSERT was
refused because its field selectStmt ends in Stmt like a statement kind, so the rule now wants a capital first.

A deep tree costs the parser's WASM about 150 KB per stack overflow (44 MB over 300 in a probe), and the parser answered
correctly after them; the tool's limit of 20,000 characters already reaches that depth, one call at a time.

Ruling: a text holding a NUL or half a surrogate pair is refused with kind syntax, beyond the plan - why: libpg-query's copy stops at a NUL and loses its end after a lone surrogate, as lengthBytesUTF8 counts it short, while pg sends every byte, so 'SELECT 1 /*...*/;COMMIT;' parsed as one SELECT and passed the check until this guard, seen red - cost if wrong: a text nobody writes on purpose is refused.
Ruling: INTO and any statement but the four are refused at every level of the tree, not only at the top as the plan's interface reads - why: Postgres takes INTO on the leftmost branch of a UNION and MERGE inside WITH, and ruling 4 refuses both - cost if wrong: none, ruling 4 leaves such text no use.
Ruling: net or net_local counts as a qualifier at any place in a function's name - why: Postgres takes db.net.f() when db is the database it runs in - cost if wrong: none.
Ruling: every failure of the parser is SqlRefused kind syntax, not only its SqlError - why: an empty text throws a plain Error and a deep tree a RangeError - cost if wrong: a parser that failed to load reads as a syntax refusal, which still runs nothing.
Ruling: tables in net and net_local stay with the database's rights, and the check refuses only calls there - why: the plan's interface says "any call", and its real tests expect 42501 for those tables - cost if wrong: none for access, as the rights hold; only the words of the refusal differ.
Ruling: REFUSED_FUNCTIONS lives in sql-check.ts, while the plan's interface and plan 4 name agent-sql.ts - why: the build lead's note, one list and no second copy - cost if wrong: a later task imports it from another file, or agent-sql.ts re-exports it.

